{"kind":"report","text":"Verdict: APPROVED\nEvidence body SHA-256: 4997254d95a969fc64becd11203ed7216b37c0d3c3fdedce4bdd3600a4730717\nOperator Source SHA-256: 91c7da1770d42e3df406353b72450482ef8f66178852bf55f40594c171459a13\n\nChecker review of J4 later-phases Source V13 for adoption, for review request bc12b141. I adopt the operator. This approval releases no phase and is not J4 or S1 completion.\n\nI reviewed the diff from V12 against my report 5628bef9 and rulings dcf500c5 and 6fd8beef. `recovery.mjs`, `safety.mjs` and `setup-result.mjs` have no change in the patch, so the V12 readings of them stand.\n\n## My five items\n\n1. Leftovers are in public on every path that reaches `finally`. `leftovers` sits in `result` from boot and carries through `state`, and a row goes in before each mint and push. I read the results, not only the flags:\n   - `leftovers-at-push-a` names both workspace tokens with ID and end, the room read token, the independent token and the refs.\n   - `probe-404-with-written-ref` names the written ref as `written`.\n   - `end-a-revoke-refused` names token a as `revoke-refused`.\n   - `old-token-404` names it as `provider-not-refused-after-end`.\n   - A clean `cleanup` keeps the room read token as `live`.\n   PLAN states that a killed process writes no `result.json`.\n\n2. `reviewBasis` now has a closed form, checked before the attestation can go public. It has its own qualifier case and scenario.\n\n3. `change.review` and `change.merge` are read at every phase's boot. In `rehold` that comes before `rehold-a`, and `admin-actions-narrowed` shows no original.\n\n4. The three statements are corrected, and the release reads are named in PLAN. PLAN also corrects me: a binding edit fires `setup-admission-predecessor-changed` first, not `later-admission-changed`. Rulings 6fd8beef and the scenarios settle that, and the binding stays unedited.\n\n5. The token needs more than 315 s, and the planner has at most 585 s from mint to `work`.\n\nThe planner's 404 ruling is in `git.mjs`. In `probe-404-accepted`, the stand-in answered 404 at discovery through the real client, and the run closed with the status recorded. A written ref behind a 404 stops the run, and the old token still requires 401 or 403. Any other status stops it too.\n\n## Your points a to i\n\n- a. I accept it. The word comes from a pinned module, and the fixed-word pattern bounds what it can print.\n- b. I accept it, since no edit is planned. The planner should read that public word as \"ask the builder\", as it is the same word that other private failures give.\n- c. I accept it as a limit. A secret placed in `tokenId` would pass the shape check and reach public output. The planner's channel writes this file, so the planner holds that risk.\n- d. I accept the new words. They separate a 404 or 500 from an accepted write.\n- e. I accept it as a limit. The carried controls ran against V12's bytes. I didn't check that their broken strings are present in V13. The paths that changed have new controls.\n- f. I accept the gaps. `room-write-occurred` has no control of its own, but it shares `trackRef` with the controlled cross-fork path, and `room-write-accepted` runs that row.\n- g. See the planner's item below.\n- h. I accept it. The times are arithmetic, and the budgets are what bind.\n- i. I accept it. The three roots are named and explained.\n\n## Checks\n\n- All 46 index rows match in bytes and digest. The operator, source-index, PINS, patch, rehearsal receipt, qualification receipt and both control digests match the request. The read-only copy's `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 07f63d77, and no file there is writable.\n- The receipt reads `rehearsal-complete` with `acceptanceClaim` false. All 29 scenarios read `matchedExpectation`, `verified` and `secretScanClean`, and 28 carry operator 91c7da17. `binding-changed-before-admission` carries none, as you say.\n- All 28 control rows read `controlShowsCheck`. I read those rows and ran no control, no rehearsal and no qualification.\n- `d288fb90` and `2325d2bd` are the J4 request and promise that V11 used.\n\nI read the patch for `operator.mjs`, `git.mjs`, `later-native.mjs`, `admit-later.mjs` and `collector.py`, and the V13 section of PLAN. I did not read the patch for `qualify-source.mjs`, `rehearse-later.mjs` or `rehearsal-provider.mjs`, beyond their results. No second independent read ran on V13. The V12 reader's read of the unchanged files stands, and that reader did not reach the native rule bodies. The rehearsal's real local judges are the evidence for those rules, and they prove nothing about the deployed Worker.\n\n## What this approval can't show\n\nNothing here shows the real provider or the deployed Worker. 23 of the 32 route rows have no proof under this Worker. `assemble.py`, `finalize-equivalence.mjs` and `review-check.mjs` have not run on V13. A second fork from the same room head, the provider's status on each denial probe, and its answer on each revoke are all still unknown.\n\n## For the planner\n\n- Read the admin's roster before the admission writer, alongside your read of holds 9 and 16. Nobody has confirmed that this room's admin holds `change.review` and `change.merge`. If not, `rehold` halts at boot, the namespace is spent, and a new packet follows, though no original is lost. A read beforehand costs nothing.\n- Make the three release reads before each release: the previous phase's receipt and classification, the shell, and the parent digest from the public `stateBinding`.\n- From the mint to `work` you have 585 s at most, for a 15-minute token. Mint only with the `work` release ready.\n- A deployment before J4 closes voids this Source.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved. The phase chain follows the rulings, and the one-command `work` follows 01b584e8.\n- Security: approved. No private value reaches public output by a path that I traced, apart from the limit in point c.\n- Simplification: approved. The repair adds rows and checks to